1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,520
Ray Dalio says America has three years.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:06,590
Forty trillion dollars of debt. More than a trillion a year,

3
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:07,560
just in interest.

4
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:11,480
He first said three years in March twenty twenty-five.

5
00:00:11,530 --> 00:00:15,840
Nineteen months later, he said it again. Three years.

6
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:19,180
So is he right? Or is the clock just moving with him?

7
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,480
We checked his warning, sign by sign,

8
00:00:21,510 --> 00:00:23,300
against the data and against history.

9
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:27,030
This is The Arab Ledger. I'm Tall. Let's go.

10
00:00:28,070 --> 00:00:29,960
First, why people listen to him.

11
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:33,810
Ray Dalio founded Bridgewater, one of the biggest hedge funds in the

12
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:34,390
world.

13
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:37,580
In two thousand and eight, while most funds lost money,

14
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:38,920
his made money.

15
00:00:39,230 --> 00:00:42,270
But he's not always right. In January twenty twenty,

16
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:46,860
he said cash is trash. Weeks later, Covid crashed the markets,

17
00:00:46,890 --> 00:00:47,940
and cash was king.

18
00:00:48,350 --> 00:00:50,910
So we don't trust the name. We check the claim.

19
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,550
Here's what he actually said.

20
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:56,280
In Dubai, in February twenty twenty-five,

21
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:58,610
he warned of a heart attack of the bond market.

22
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:02,450
A few weeks later, on Bloomberg: I can't tell you exactly when.

23
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:04,500
My guess would be three years,

24
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:05,350
give or take a year.

25
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:09,920
And in October twenty twenty-six: a debt crisis within the next three

26
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:13,330
years. America is approaching our limits.

27
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:17,720
His theory has five warning signs. We tested every one.

28
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:22,240
Sign one. Is the debt growing faster than the money coming in?

29
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:27,040
This year, the US government took in five point four trillion dollars.

30
00:01:27,210 --> 00:01:28,480
It spent seven point four.

31
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:32,560
The gap: almost two trillion dollars. In one year.

32
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:34,780
With no war at home and no recession.

33
00:01:35,210 --> 00:01:38,720
Spending grew six percent. Income grew three.

34
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:41,730
The debt the public holds is now about the size of the

35
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:42,890
whole US economy.

36
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:44,220
Verdict: true.

37
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,550
Sign two. Is interest starting to squeeze everything else?

38
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:53,180
This year, interest on the debt cost more than a trillion dollars.

39
00:01:53,210 --> 00:01:55,270
About three billion dollars a day.

40
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:58,290
That's more than America spent on its entire military.

41
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:01,840
Nineteen cents of every tax dollar now goes to interest,

42
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:04,410
before a single road or soldier is paid for.

43
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:09,160
And it compounds. The government's own budget office expects interest to hit

44
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:11,770
two point one trillion a year within ten years.

45
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:16,070
Verdict: true. This is the part that worries Dalio most,

46
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:17,560
and the numbers back him.

47
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:22,120
Sign three. Is there more debt for sale than people who want

48
00:02:22,150 --> 00:02:22,610
to buy it?

49
00:02:23,210 --> 00:02:27,570
Dalio says the US will sell about two point one trillion dollars

50
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:28,600
of new debt this year.

51
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:30,100
Someone has to buy it.

52
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:33,590
In two thousand and eight, foreigners held more than half of America's

53
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:36,180
debt. Today, about thirty-one percent.

54
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:39,960
China has been cutting. In twenty twenty-five,

55
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:42,530
America lost its last top credit rating.

56
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:46,990
But the buyers haven't left. Foreigners hold more US debt in dollars

57
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,840
than ever. Japan alone holds over a trillion.

58
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,900
What changed is the price. They want more interest to keep buying.

59
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:57,550
The ten-year yield has been near five percent.

60
00:02:58,010 --> 00:03:02,480
Verdict: partly true. The buyers are still there. They're just getting expensive.

61
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:07,920
Sign four. Does debt this big always end in a crisis?

62
00:03:08,290 --> 00:03:09,770
This is where history helps.

63
00:03:10,170 --> 00:03:14,010
Japan owes almost twice the size of its economy. No crisis.

64
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:15,140
For decades.

65
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:19,520
Why? It borrows in its own currency, mostly from its own savers.

66
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:23,230
America has been here before too. After World War Two,

67
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:25,130
its debt was bigger than its economy.

68
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:29,420
It never defaulted. Growth and inflation shrank the debt over thirty years.

69
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:31,070
Quietly. Savers paid the bill.

70
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:36,970
Now look at Britain in twenty twenty-two. A surprise budget,

71
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,840
and bond markets panicked in days.

72
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:42,690
The Bank of England had to step in.

73
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:45,480
The prime minister was gone in forty-five days.

74
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,630
That's what a bond-market heart attack looks like. It can happen fast.

75
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:51,010
Even to a rich country.

76
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:55,470
And Greece, in twenty ten, borrowed in euros it couldn't print.

77
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:55,030
It needed a bailout.

78
00:03:55,060 --> 00:04:01,910
Verdict: it depends. Countries that print their own money rarely default.

79
00:04:02,070 --> 00:04:02,780
They inflate.

80
00:04:03,170 --> 00:04:05,030
And America prints the world's money.

81
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:08,260
Sign five. The timing.

82
00:04:08,825 --> 00:04:11,965
Dalio's first guess, three years give or take one,

83
00:04:12,145 --> 00:04:14,815
points to twenty twenty-seven to twenty twenty-nine.

84
00:04:15,265 --> 00:04:18,155
His new one says by twenty twenty-nine. To be fair,

85
00:04:18,335 --> 00:04:20,165
that's still inside his first range.

86
00:04:20,595 --> 00:04:23,315
But he said it himself: I can't tell you exactly when.

87
00:04:23,395 --> 00:04:24,375
It's like a heart attack.

88
00:04:24,745 --> 00:04:27,275
You know the arteries are clogged. You don't know the day.

89
00:04:27,695 --> 00:04:32,025
Verdict: unproven. The direction is right. The date is a guess.

90
00:04:33,105 --> 00:04:34,805
So, the final score.

91
00:04:35,105 --> 00:04:39,445
Debt growing faster than income: true. Interest squeezing the budget: true.

92
00:04:39,975 --> 00:04:44,585
Not enough buyers: partly. Big debt always ends in crisis: it depends.

93
00:04:44,745 --> 00:04:46,505
Three years: unproven.

94
00:04:46,985 --> 00:04:50,555
Verdict: Dalio's math is right. His clock is a guess.

95
00:04:51,615 --> 00:04:53,075
But here's what most people miss.

96
00:04:53,455 --> 00:04:57,965
When people hear debt crisis, they picture a crash. Banks closing.

97
00:04:58,055 --> 00:04:59,585
A day everything breaks.

98
00:05:00,095 --> 00:05:02,015
For a country that prints its own money,

99
00:05:02,055 --> 00:05:03,865
the crisis usually looks different.

100
00:05:04,195 --> 00:05:07,035
It looks like your money buying less, year after year.

101
00:05:07,475 --> 00:05:12,745
After World War Two, America didn't crash. It inflated. The debt shrank,

102
00:05:12,825 --> 00:05:13,725
and so did savings.

103
00:05:14,205 --> 00:05:18,115
So the real question isn't will America go broke? It's who pays?

104
00:05:18,255 --> 00:05:20,695
And the answer is usually: anyone holding cash.

105
00:05:21,715 --> 00:05:25,735
So if the bill always comes, how do countries actually pay it?

106
00:05:26,085 --> 00:05:27,615
History shows three ways out.

107
00:05:28,655 --> 00:05:31,095
Way one: the painful one. Cut.

108
00:05:32,155 --> 00:05:36,545
In January nineteen ninety-five, the Wall Street Journal called Canada an honorary

109
00:05:36,575 --> 00:05:37,845
member of the Third World.

110
00:05:38,875 --> 00:05:41,945
Its federal debt was close to seventy percent of its economy.

111
00:05:43,005 --> 00:05:45,465
A month later, the finance minister, Paul Martin,

112
00:05:45,555 --> 00:05:47,825
delivered a budget that cut spending hard.

113
00:05:48,225 --> 00:05:51,215
About seven dollars of cuts for every dollar of new taxes.

114
00:05:52,295 --> 00:05:56,885
Within three years, Canada had a surplus. By two thousand and nine,

115
00:05:56,945 --> 00:05:58,975
its federal debt was down to twenty-nine percent.

116
00:06:00,015 --> 00:06:02,885
It worked. But it hurt. The provinces,

117
00:06:02,915 --> 00:06:04,975
hospitals and public workers paid for it.

118
00:06:06,015 --> 00:06:08,425
Way two: the lucky one. Grow.

119
00:06:09,455 --> 00:06:13,515
In the nineteen-nineties, America raised some taxes, held back spending,

120
00:06:13,695 --> 00:06:15,985
and then the tech boom poured money into the budget.

121
00:06:17,045 --> 00:06:21,505
From nineteen ninety-eight to two thousand and one, the US ran surpluses.

122
00:06:21,885 --> 00:06:23,805
The first since nineteen sixty-nine.

123
00:06:25,045 --> 00:06:27,915
The debt the public held fell from about forty-eight percent of the

124
00:06:27,945 --> 00:06:29,375
economy to thirty-one.

125
00:06:30,425 --> 00:06:33,505
Growth did the heavy lifting. But that needs a boom,

126
00:06:33,535 --> 00:06:34,825
and a Congress that agrees.

127
00:06:35,885 --> 00:06:38,555
Way three: the ugly one. Ask for help.

128
00:06:39,615 --> 00:06:44,395
In nineteen seventy-six, Britain's inflation had been close to twenty-five percent,

129
00:06:44,575 --> 00:06:46,195
and the pound hit a record low.

130
00:06:47,275 --> 00:06:51,325
It asked the International Monetary Fund for a three point nine billion

131
00:06:51,355 --> 00:06:54,645
dollar loan. The largest the fund had ever been asked for.

132
00:06:55,755 --> 00:06:59,535
In return, Britain had to cut spending anyway. On someone else's terms.

133
00:07:00,595 --> 00:07:03,105
It paid the loan back by nineteen seventy-nine.

134
00:07:03,455 --> 00:07:06,705
But going to the IMF became a national humiliation.

135
00:07:07,775 --> 00:07:10,765
And there's a fourth way. The quiet one we just talked about.

136
00:07:10,795 --> 00:07:11,465
Inflate.

137
00:07:12,505 --> 00:07:14,315
So which way will America take?

138
00:07:15,385 --> 00:07:19,555
Cutting like Canada needs both parties to agree on cuts and taxes.

139
00:07:19,795 --> 00:07:21,235
That's rare in Washington.

140
00:07:22,285 --> 00:07:24,085
Growing like the nineties needs a boom.

141
00:07:24,385 --> 00:07:27,185
Some say AI could be that boom. That's a big bet.

142
00:07:28,275 --> 00:07:31,015
And the IMF can't bail out the country that prints the world's

143
00:07:31,045 --> 00:07:31,445
money.

144
00:07:32,395 --> 00:07:34,995
But the person behind this channel sees a fifth way.

145
00:07:35,145 --> 00:07:36,135
And it's already law.

146
00:07:37,165 --> 00:07:40,965
In July twenty twenty-five, America passed the GENIUS Act.

147
00:07:41,115 --> 00:07:45,015
Dollar stablecoins must now be backed one to one by safe assets,

148
00:07:45,045 --> 00:07:47,495
like cash and short-term US Treasury bills.

149
00:07:48,565 --> 00:07:52,415
So when someone in Lagos, Cairo or Casablanca holds a digital dollar

150
00:07:52,445 --> 00:07:55,805
from a big American company, part of that money is lending to

151
00:07:55,835 --> 00:07:56,695
the US Treasury.

152
00:07:57,725 --> 00:08:02,245
America's Treasury Secretary has said he expects stablecoins to become a big

153
00:08:02,275 --> 00:08:03,955
new buyer of US debt.

154
00:08:05,005 --> 00:08:09,025
His view: that's how America gets the whole world to fund its

155
00:08:09,055 --> 00:08:12,725
debt. Quietly. One phone wallet at a time.

156
00:08:13,785 --> 00:08:17,449
So the likely path isn't one way. It's the quiet two together.

157
00:08:17,729 --> 00:08:20,395
A little more inflation, and the world lending through stablecoins.

158
00:08:21,445 --> 00:08:24,865
That's why, for your money, the twist matters more than the clock.

159
00:08:25,916 --> 00:08:28,216
Here's how it looks from our side of the world.

160
00:08:28,626 --> 00:08:30,776
Dalio first gave this warning in Dubai.

161
00:08:31,026 --> 00:08:32,386
And he picked the right room.

162
00:08:32,806 --> 00:08:36,546
Saudi Arabia holds about a hundred and forty billion dollars of US

163
00:08:36,576 --> 00:08:39,526
debt. The UAE, about a hundred and nineteen.

164
00:08:39,856 --> 00:08:41,546
But the bigger link is the peg.

165
00:08:41,986 --> 00:08:45,826
The Saudi riyal has been tied to the dollar since nineteen eighty-six.

166
00:08:46,086 --> 00:08:48,826
The UAE dirham since nineteen ninety-seven.

167
00:08:49,296 --> 00:08:51,466
So when US interest rates rise,

168
00:08:51,576 --> 00:08:54,586
mortgages in Riyadh and Dubai rise with them.

169
00:08:55,036 --> 00:08:57,056
Whether their economies need it or not.

170
00:08:57,476 --> 00:09:00,416
Even Morocco's dirham is forty percent dollar.

171
00:09:00,876 --> 00:09:03,076
If America pays its debt with inflation,

172
00:09:03,276 --> 00:09:05,136
part of that bill gets mailed to us.

173
00:09:05,536 --> 00:09:08,426
And one more thing, from the person behind this channel.

174
00:09:08,896 --> 00:09:13,446
His take: most of this is noise. The dollar isn't going away.

175
00:09:13,636 --> 00:09:15,746
It may change shape, maybe go digital,

176
00:09:15,806 --> 00:09:17,916
but it will still be America's money.

177
00:09:18,336 --> 00:09:19,956
So instead of fearing the debt,

178
00:09:20,036 --> 00:09:22,546
he'd own assets that can't simply be printed.

179
00:09:23,006 --> 00:09:25,276
For him, that means Bitcoin and Eth.

180
00:09:25,876 --> 00:09:29,126
Even Dalio says he holds some bitcoin, but not much.

181
00:09:29,436 --> 00:09:30,806
Though he prefers gold.

182
00:09:31,296 --> 00:09:34,116
He also thinks AI agents will one day need money of their

183
00:09:34,146 --> 00:09:35,836
own, and that they'll pay in crypto.

184
00:09:36,336 --> 00:09:40,106
Today, most of them pay in digital dollars, called stablecoins.

185
00:09:40,136 --> 00:09:41,976
He's betting that changes.

186
00:09:42,376 --> 00:09:46,086
That's his view, not advice. Crypto can lose half its value in

187
00:09:46,116 --> 00:09:46,556
a year.

188
00:09:47,636 --> 00:09:48,976
So what do you do with this?

189
00:09:50,516 --> 00:09:53,776
One: cash sitting still is the asset that loses most when debt

190
00:09:53,806 --> 00:09:57,606
is paid with inflation. Know what your savings earn after inflation.

191
00:09:57,926 --> 00:10:01,756
Two: watch the ten-year yield. It's the bond market's heart rate.

192
00:10:02,086 --> 00:10:03,736
If it jumps fast, pay attention.

193
00:10:04,176 --> 00:10:08,316
Three: if you borrow, a fixed rate protects you from a world

194
00:10:08,346 --> 00:10:09,426
where rates stay high.

195
00:10:09,846 --> 00:10:13,006
This is education, not financial advice.

196
00:10:14,106 --> 00:10:15,716
So we'll keep an eye on Dalio's clock.

197
00:10:16,196 --> 00:10:19,826
By his own words, the crisis should show up by twenty twenty-nine.

198
00:10:20,056 --> 00:10:20,646
We'll be here.

199
00:10:21,176 --> 00:10:24,166
Your turn: is Dalio right, early, or wrong?

200
00:10:24,306 --> 00:10:25,196
Tell us in the comments.

201
00:10:26,256 --> 00:10:28,766
Subscribe, so you see what happens next.

202
00:10:29,136 --> 00:10:30,846
Same world. Different lens.
